ficantly reduce wait times. It's crucial to track resource usage and optimize command buffer management to avoid idling during computation.**Analyzing Thunderbolt performance and optimization strategies**\n\nI need to discuss Thunderbolt link capabilities, particularly the differences between 20Gb/s and 40Gb/s. To optimize performance, I’m considering the effects of streaming data and how memory management affects GPU workloads. Utilizing page caching could significantly improve efficiency. We should also think about eliminating NOCACHE to enhance performance further. Adopting triple buffering and MTLHeaps could reduce overhead while ensuring effective use of memory for weights. Additionally, rewriting attention mechanisms in shaders and using more efficient data handling might yield substantial gains. Overall, optimizing these processes could significantly cut GPU time and enhance throughput.**Optimizing GPU activation handling**\n\nIt seems the actual significant waiting time happens when the GPU writes activations to Shared memory over TB3, throttling overall compute throughput and causing stalls. This issue arises because activations need to be converted to Private memory. By implementing \"ping-pong\" buffers for each tensor shape and using MTLHeap to manage allocations, we can greatly enhance efficiency. Additionally, moving all activations and weights to Private allows better VRAM management, enabling faster processing. This change could significantly reduce GPU time during operations, making the pipeline much smoother and quicker.**Improving GPU blitting efficiency**\n\nI'm focusing on the synchronous blit process to create an efficient pipeline using command queues and shared events. Each activation must transition to Private memory to avoid bottlenecks, as using Shared for activations can severely slow down performance. I also need to restructure the kernel optimizations to improve throughput, particularly by utilizing the Infinity Cache on RDNA2.\n\nLike,